Secretary of State Rex Tillerson says  the u.s. is still interested in a  dialogue with North Korea but it is up  to Kim jong-un to make that happen  thousands of protesters yesterday took  to the streets of Seoul the capital of  South Korea they demanded a peaceful  solution to the nuclear confrontation  with North Korea Ben Tracy is in Seoul  with why demonstrators are also calling  on President Trump to tone down his  rhetoric  Ben good morning a good morning so  people here in Seoul are used to dealing  with the North Korean threat but they're  increasingly worried that this verbal  sparring match between president Trump  and Kim jong-un could eventually lead to  an actual war and they would pay the  price but now it looks like both sides  are trying to find a way to speak a bit  more constructively if North Korea ends  its seemingly endless barrage of missile  tests Washington says it's willing to  negotiate with Pyongyang  especially after kim jeong-hoon decided  to hold off on launching missiles  towards guam we would like to have talks  with him when the time is right when  they show that they are serious serious  about an effort to move toward  denuclearization we have not seen that  yet  all sides seem to be acknowledging the  obvious a war on the Korean Peninsula  would be catastrophic this is the border  between South and North Korea and just  over there North Korea has thousands of  pieces of artillery pointed at the south  it's estimated if they were to unleash  those sixty four thousand people would  die in South Korea in just the first day  the u.s. seems to be backing down from  its demand that North Korea abandon its  weapons program entirely before talks  could begin saying rather that North  Korea needs to freeze its weapons  testing for a period of time there was  some effort I think to recognize North  Korea's security concerns and sort of  meet them halfway john delorean of  international studies at Yonsei  University in Seoul I don't think the  relationship that the North Koreans want  is screaming back and forth but they'll  take that I think Kim jong-un is more  ambitious I think things like a summit  with Donald Trump and the Selphy that  the world is waiting for between these  two extraordinary figures is the kind of  thing that they'd be interested in China  and Russia have floated a plan by which  North Korea would freeze its missile  tests in exchange for the u.s. in South  Korea freezing their joint military  exercises while the US government says  that's a non-starter and those military  exercises will take place as planned  next week here in Seoul Gayle Thank You  Ben Tracy reporting from Seoul
 

